{"name":"Chapel Blue","hex":"#b0d2e7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chapel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place of worship, smaller than or subordinate to a church.","A place of worship in another building or within a civil institution such as a larger church, airport, prison, monastery, school, etc.; often primarily for private prayer."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Describing a person who attends a nonconformist chapel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chapel"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chapel","blocks":[{"text":"A chapel is a Christian place of prayer and worship that is usually relatively small. The term has several meanings. First, smaller spaces inside a church that have their own altar are often called chapels; the Lady chapel is a common type of these. Second, a chapel is a place of worship, sometimes interfaith, that is part of a building, complex, or vessel with some other main purpose, such as a school, college, hospital, palace or large aristocratic house, castle, barracks, prison, funeral home, cemetery, hotel, airport, or military or commercial ship. Third, chapels are small places of worship, built as satellite sites by a church or monastery, for example in remote areas; these are often called a chapel of ease. A feature of all these types is that often no clergy are permanently resident or specifically attached to the chapel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapel","wikidata":"Q108325"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/1280px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic rendering of typical \"side chapels\" in the apse of a cathedral, surrounding the ambulatory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApsidal_chapels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/500px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/1280px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/500px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Tsrviz Chapel in Armenia, one of the oldest chapels in the world","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Chapelle_Palatine.jpg/500px-Chapelle_Palatine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Chapelle_Palatine.jpg/500px-Chapelle_Palatine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Cappella Palatina in Palermo, Italy (pictured), and the Palatine Chapel in Aachen, two of the most famous palace chapels of Europe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChapelle_Palatine.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/500px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/1280px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg/500px-Capel_Salem%2C_Pwllheli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Capel Salem, a nonconformist chapel in Pwllheli, Wales. Unlike earlier types of chapel, this chapel is not attached to a larger place of worship.","link":"https: